Approach
1) Build data index
- Load all CSVs into memory (pandas or csv module)
- Index users by email (current), also build reverse maps from historical identifiers found in audit log / other evidence
- Build user identity graph: for each user_id collect all known emails/phones/name/email_hash values across time (current from user_identities + all audit log entries for that user_id) - needed because request email might be historical
- Load deletion_rules into a lookup structure
- Load system_activity (index by resource_id, by time ranges)
- Load evidence_files manifest with full paths (resolve relative to dataset root)
2) Ingest and parse all evidence
We need to scan every evidence file under dataset/evidence/ (respecting captured_at vs audit_at - must not use evidence captured after case's audit_at). Parse by type:
- SQL files (.sql): SQLite-compatible snapshots with customer_data_primary and possibly replicas; contains JSON in record_json with resource_id, user_id, email, phone, email_hash etc. Need to query all relevant tables, track snapshot time.
- JSON files (.json): bundles/snapshots with records
- TXT files (.txt) like handover docs
- LOG files (.log): structured logs with timestamps, trace_ids, resource_ids, events, match_key containing emails/ids
- PDF files (.pdf): business artifacts (invoices, support, handover) - need text extraction (e.g. PyPDF2/pdfplumber/pymupdf). Images may be referenced; also PNGs exist - may contain screenshots of consoles (names/ids/emails visible). Need OCR if text not extractable? Or maybe look up if any libs available; better to write a robust parser that tries text extraction first.
- PNG/JPG images: may need OCR (pytesseract) if text present as pixels, but environment might not have tesseract installed. Need to be defensive - try to extract any embedded text or metadata; also filenames/origins give hints.
Build a unified evidence index: for each extracted fact record associations like (email, user_id, resource_id, system_id, data_category/type hints, timestamps, file_id, raw_snippet). This is the core of connecting evidence to cases.
3) For each case, determine if deletion authorized (NOT_APPLICABLE)
Analyze request_text (multilingual - EN/ES/FR/JA etc as seen in samples). Look for deletion intent: "delete", "erase", "close account", "remove profile", "suppression", "eliminar", "supprimer", "削除" etc. Requests for access/correction/export/marketing opt-out are NOT_APPLICABLE. Also "download your data" alone isn't deletion request. Need to classify intent. Can do keyword/regex based classification - should be straightforward and deterministic.
If not authorized -> status NOT_APPLICABLE, steps_json []
4) Resolve identities (current + historical)
For authorized cases:
- Get request email from case
- Find matching user in user_identities by current email (case-insensitive)
- If not found directly, look up in audit log: find any audit entries where old_value/new_value matches the request email (or phone) and get associated user_id; also build full identity timeline for candidate users
- Collect all known identifiers for the subject user_id(s): all emails (current + historical from audit + from evidence), phones, name variants, email_hash, user_id itself
- Also need to consider the case says "I used this Yahoo address before changing the email on my account" etc - so must be thorough
5) Reconstruct state at audit_at and find relevant records/risks
For the subject identities, search across:
- Evidence index (all parsed evidence) for mentions of any identifier/resource tied to subject
- system_activity.csv for events involving subject identifiers/resources (filter by event_time <= audit_at)
- Look up by user_id, emails, phones, email_hash, resource_ids that appear linked
Identify all resources that could be relevant per deletion_rules categories (USER_PROFILE, SECONDARY_IDENTIFIER, SEARCH_INDEX_ENTRY, CACHE_ENTRY, SUPPORT_MESSAGE, SUPPORT_ATTACHMENT, ANALYTICS_PROFILE, PRODUCT_TELEMETRY, USER_EXPORT, MARKETING_PROFILE, EMAIL_DELIVERY_LOG, SECURITY_AUDIT_LOG, LEGAL_HOLD_RECORD, BACKUP_ARCHIVE, ACCOUNT_TOMBSTONE). Also look for active data flows/integrations and restore risks (backups).
For each relevant resource found, determine its current state at audit_at from evidence (treat activity/workflow statuses as claims). Apply deletion_rules:
- Determine required action based on data_category and conditions (e.g. SUPPORT_MESSAGE with shared context must be retained but REDACTED; SECURITY_AUDIT_LOG within retention period -> RETAIN; ACCOUNT_TOMBSTONE with only stable user_id needed -> REDACT per rule_15)
- Check if properly treated: deleted/absent, redacted (no prohibited identifiers), anonymized, or retained as required
Also need to find recreation risks: active sync/import/data flows (integration_service data_flow_*) that can copy/recreate data; backups that can restore (backup_service). These are findings as REPORT_ACTIVE_DATA_FLOW / REPORT_RESTORE_RISK.
6) Decide COMPLETE vs INCOMPLETE
Deletion is COMPLETE only if:
- every relevant copy has received treatment required by deletion_rules.csv
- no active sync/import/restore path can recreate the data  
- retained records contain only identifiers permitted by rules
- records that must be retained remain available
Also tombstones: preserve stable internal user_id, remove name/email/phone/email_hash and other direct identifiers as required.
If ANY relevant record not properly treated OR active recreation path exists that isn't mitigated OR retained record has prohibited identifiers exposed -> INCOMPLETE. Otherwise COMPLETE (if authorized).
7) Generate steps_json with correct ordering
Phase order: FINDING, then REMEDIATION, then VERIFICATION (globally). Within each phase, follow operation order as given in tables, followed by system_id ascending, then resource_id ascending.
For INCOMPLETE:
- FINDING phase: cite evidence (use actual file_id/activity_id/audit_id that proves). Operations: REPORT_REMAINING_RECORD (PRESENT), REPORT_EXPOSED_PII (PRESENT), REPORT_ACTIVE_DATA_FLOW (ACTIVE), REPORT_RESTORE_RISK (ACTIVE)
- REMEDIATION phase: proposed actions with evidence_id="" (empty string). Order: within remediation, disable data flows and block unsafe restores BEFORE changing dependent records. Process downstream copies before their source records when dependency requires. Operations: DISABLE_DATA_FLOW (DISABLED), BLOCK_RESTORE (BLOCKED), DELETE_RECORD (ABSENT), REDACT_RECORD (REDACTED), ANONYMIZE_RECORD (ANONYMIZED)
- VERIFICATION phase: evidence_id=""
For COMPLETE: only VERIFICATION steps proving current compliant state. Must cite evidence (file_id/activity_id/audit_id). Operations as per verification table.
For NOT_APPLICABLE: []
Need to map found resources to (system_id, resource_id, data category) correctly. The samples show patterns like account_service/account_XXX, search_service/search_XXX, support_platform/support_XXX, analytics_platform/analytics_XXX, backup_service/backup_XXX, integration_service/data_flow_XXX, export_service/export_XXX, security_service/audit_record_XXX, identity_service/tombstone_XXX etc.
Also "within remediation, disable data flows and block unsafe restores before changing dependent records" - so DISABLE_DATA_FLOW/BLOCK_RESTORE come before DELETE/REDACT/ANONYMIZE of dependent records.
